<div class="footnotes" role="doc-endnotes">
  <ol>
    <li id="fn:1" role="doc-endnote">
      <p>While it may be common in practice to assume your data comes from a distribution with finite variance, this is not always the case. For example, both stock market fluctuations and extreme weather events may have extreme events that occur more often than predicted by a Gaussian distribution. In these cases, when your variance is unbounded, the central limit theorem does not apply. <a href="#fnref:1" class="reversefootnote" role="doc-backlink">&#8617;</a></p>
    </li>
    <li id="fn:2" role="doc-endnote">
      <p>In fact, the Cauchy distribution doesn’t have a variance <em>or</em> a mean! <a href="#fnref:2" class="reversefootnote" role="doc-backlink">&#8617;</a></p>
    </li>
    <li id="fn:3" role="doc-endnote">
      <p>The family of “stable” distributions are so-called because if \(X_1, \ldots, X_N\) are samples from a stable distribution with some parameters, then the sum \(\sum_{i=1}^N X_i\) also has a stable distribution. This means you can think of a stable distribution as a sort of “attractor” for sums of random variables. <a href="#fnref:3" class="reversefootnote" role="doc-backlink">&#8617;</a></p>
    </li>
  </ol>
</div>
